1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,431
Alex Morgan's contract ended Friday.

2
00:00:02,431 --> 00:00:07,407
It's Tuesday, and Alex can still ship code
to production and export customer data.

3
00:00:07,407 --> 00:00:11,667
Nobody has noticed, because that access
isn't on Alex's profile.

4
00:00:11,667 --> 00:00:12,685
It's inherited.

5
00:00:12,685 --> 00:00:15,139
Every offboarding team knows this problem.

6
00:00:15,139 --> 00:00:19,329
The truth is buried in an access console
nobody has time to read.

7
00:00:19,329 --> 00:00:21,921
And pasting screenshots into a chat window

8
00:00:21,921 --> 00:00:24,514
gets you a guess, not a path you can
defend.

9
00:00:24,514 --> 00:00:28,484
So this page hands the agent real tools
instead of pixels.

10
00:00:28,484 --> 00:00:32,512
Nine WebMCP tools, registered right in the
document.

11
00:00:32,512 --> 00:00:33,345
Resolve.

12
00:00:33,345 --> 00:00:34,225
Trace.

13
00:00:34,225 --> 00:00:35,104
Simulate.

14
00:00:35,104 --> 00:00:35,856
Preview.

15
00:00:35,856 --> 00:00:36,782
Stage.

16
00:00:36,782 --> 00:00:41,505
Codex discovers all nine, and one sentence
starts the review.

17
00:00:41,505 --> 00:00:43,391
From here I don't touch the interface.

18
00:00:43,391 --> 00:00:46,023
Codex resolves the identity, computes

19
00:00:46,023 --> 00:00:49,213
effective access, and the map redraws
itself.

20
00:00:49,213 --> 00:00:50,602
Four separate paths.

21
00:00:50,602 --> 00:00:52,245
Six sensitive grants.

22
00:00:52,245 --> 00:00:54,502
Each one explained, not just listed.

23
00:00:54,502 --> 00:00:56,562
The interesting one is the second.

24
00:00:56,562 --> 00:01:01,863
Alex sits in Contractors, Contractors
nests into Platform Contributors,

25
00:01:01,863 --> 00:01:05,706
and that grants Release Manager, which
approves production releases.

26
00:01:05,706 --> 00:01:08,715
The membership graph even loops back on
itself.

27
00:01:08,715 --> 00:01:12,488
The traversal catches the cycle instead of
counting it twice.

28
00:01:12,488 --> 00:01:14,699
Now the part a checklist gets wrong.

29
00:01:14,699 --> 00:01:18,981
Codex simulates removing everything, and
the page pushes back.

30
00:01:18,981 --> 00:01:22,535
On-call Incident Relay depends on Alex's
rollback grant.

31
00:01:22,535 --> 00:01:25,671
Revoke it all, and the next outage has no
way back.

32
00:01:25,671 --> 00:01:28,067
So the plan gets revised, not abandoned.

33
00:01:28,067 --> 00:01:29,745
Five grants leave Alex.

34
00:01:29,745 --> 00:01:33,218
One shared dependency moves to a service
identity.

35
00:01:33,218 --> 00:01:35,856
Exposure falls from ninety-two to
eighteen.

36
00:01:35,856 --> 00:01:38,461
And so far, nothing has actually changed.

37
00:01:38,461 --> 00:01:40,347
Because the agent can't change it.

38
00:01:40,347 --> 00:01:41,782
Staging is a tool.

39
00:01:41,782 --> 00:01:43,449
Confirming is not.

40
00:01:43,449 --> 00:01:48,993
Codex asks for the receipt, and the page
answers plainly: no receipt exists yet.

41
00:01:48,993 --> 00:01:50,347
The last step is mine.

42
00:01:50,347 --> 00:01:52,882
One human click, one read-only receipt.

43
00:01:52,882 --> 00:01:57,662
Then Codex re-reads the live state: Alex,
zero sensitive grants.

44
00:01:57,662 --> 00:02:00,845
Rollback now owned by the on-call service
identity.

45
00:02:00,845 --> 00:02:02,847
That's what WebMCP unlocks.

46
00:02:02,847 --> 00:02:05,201
An agent reasoning inside your app, on

47
00:02:05,201 --> 00:02:08,218
your data model, with the decision still
human.

48
00:02:08,218 --> 00:02:10,150
The identities here are synthetic.

49
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:12,106
The offboarding problem is not.
